WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.900 position:50% size:92% align:center
One warp requests thirty-two FP32 values. That

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.128 position:50% size:92% align:center
is one hundred and twenty-eight useful bytes.

00:00:07.348 --> 00:00:10.128 position:50% size:92% align:center
Changing the input stride can change the number

00:00:10.128 --> 00:00:12.258 position:50% size:92% align:center
of addressed sectors even when every

00:00:12.258 --> 00:00:13.855 position:50% size:92% align:center
lane still reads one value.

00:00:15.875 --> 00:00:18.959 position:50% size:92% align:center
The warp’s threads supply addresses to the SM’s

00:00:18.959 --> 00:00:20.534 position:50% size:92% align:center
load and store hardware.

00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:23.717 position:50% size:92% align:center
The memory path groups these addresses. Caches

00:00:23.717 --> 00:00:25.585 position:50% size:92% align:center
can satisfy requests before a

00:00:25.585 --> 00:00:27.325 position:50% size:92% align:center
miss reaches device memory.

00:00:29.345 --> 00:00:32.178 position:50% size:92% align:center
With stride one, lanes zero, one and two read

00:00:32.178 --> 00:00:35.201 position:50% size:92% align:center
byte addresses zero, four and eight. Those words

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:37.153 position:50% size:92% align:center
share the first aligned sector.

00:00:37.373 --> 00:00:39.564 position:50% size:92% align:center
Across the whole warp, the thirty-two

00:00:39.564 --> 00:00:41.341 position:50% size:92% align:center
addresses occupy four sectors.

00:00:43.361 --> 00:00:46.135 position:50% size:92% align:center
Now use stride eight. Each input word is four

00:00:46.135 --> 00:00:47.861 position:50% size:92% align:center
bytes, so neighbouring lanes

00:00:47.861 --> 00:00:49.526 position:50% size:92% align:center
are thirty-two bytes apart.

00:00:49.746 --> 00:00:52.147 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause and predict whether the warp still

00:00:52.147 --> 00:00:54.188 position:50% size:92% align:center
addresses four sectors. The useful

00:00:54.188 --> 00:00:55.869 position:50% size:92% align:center
value count has not changed.

00:01:01.689 --> 00:01:04.264 position:50% size:92% align:center
Stride eight places each lane’s word in a

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:07.217 position:50% size:92% align:center
different sector. The warp addresses thirty-two

00:01:07.217 --> 00:01:09.667 position:50% size:92% align:center
sectors for the same thirty-two values.

00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:12.601 position:50% size:92% align:center
That covers one thousand and twenty-four sector

00:01:12.601 --> 00:01:15.199 position:50% size:92% align:center
bytes while only one hundred and twenty-eight

00:01:15.199 --> 00:01:17.162 position:50% size:92% align:center
bytes contain the requested words.

00:01:19.182 --> 00:01:22.072 position:50% size:92% align:center
These counts describe the addresses touched by

00:01:22.072 --> 00:01:24.900 position:50% size:92% align:center
one load instruction. They do not tell us how

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.722 position:50% size:92% align:center
many requests miss the caches

00:01:26.722 --> 00:01:28.355 position:50% size:92% align:center
or how long the GPU takes.

00:01:28.575 --> 00:01:31.761 position:50% size:92% align:center
To explain the count, follow lane addresses into

00:01:31.761 --> 00:01:34.947 position:50% size:92% align:center
aligned sectors. To explain real memory traffic,

00:01:34.947 --> 00:01:36.872 position:50% size:92% align:center
additional cache and hardware

00:01:36.872 --> 00:01:38.133 position:50% size:92% align:center
evidence is needed.
